[tool_use] Agent {"description":"Code review: Phase 2b.3 accelerometer + calibration","subagent_type":"code-reviewer","prompt":"Review the Phase 2b.3 changes on the `main` branch of `/home/aaron/dev/projects/aaron/pointfight-reactor`. This is a React+TypeScript app; Phase 2b.3 adds accelerometer-based motion-commit detection and a per-athlete calibration flow.\n\n**Context (so you don't have to re-derive it):**\n\n- The phasekit workflow runs in audit-first mode. The earlier phase (2b.2) is `git log`'s most recent approval. The full plan for 2b.3 lives in `artifacts/decision-memo.md` §\"Sub-phase 2b.3\" and §B3 (falsifiable acceptance criteria) and §B4 (Android-gated, iOS doc-driven).\n- Phase 2b.3 acceptance criteria from `docs/PHASES.md`:\n  - 60s at rest → 0 commits\n  - 5 forward-snap impulses → exactly 5 commits\n  - Double-tap within 300ms debounce → exactly 1 commit\n  - Calibration: 5 sample swings → threshold persists across reload; re-running overwrites prior value\n  - `phone/motion.ts` is pure-function unit-tested\n  - `docs/SETUP_IOS.md` documents the mkcert path (doc-driven, not CI-gated)\n  - Android Chrome over plain HTTP works without permission prompts\n  - Manual real-device QA at `app/verify-phase-2b3.mjs`\n- Per `docs/QUALITY_GATES.md` §\"Browser-API limitations (Phase 2b)\": DeviceMotion and live transport are gated by manual real-device QA, not by automated tests. Pure logic must still be unit-tested.\n\n**What I changed (review focus):**\n\nNew files:\n- `app/src/phone/motion.ts` — pure impulse detection + calibration math\n- `app/src/phone/motion-runner.ts` — pure mode-aware sample→message translator\n- `app/src/phone/motion.test.ts`, `motion-runner.test.ts` — unit tests\n- `app/src/ui/CalibrateScreen.tsx` — laptop calibration panel (mounted inside PairScreen when peer connected)\n- `docs/SETUP_IOS.md` — mkcert iOS walkthrough\n- `app/verify-phase-2b3.mjs` — Playwright verify driver with C1+C2 gate-grade checks, C3 best-effort, M1–M8 manual QA checklist\n\nModified files:\n- `app/src/phone/wire.ts` + `wire.test.ts` — extended PhoneMessage union with `sample` (phone→laptop calibration peaks) and `config` (laptop→phone threshold/mode) variants\n- `app/src/engine/types.ts` — added optional `PhoneCalibration` interface and `DrillConfig.phoneCalibration?: PhoneCalibration`\n- `app/src/store/profiles.test.ts` — round-trip tests for the new field\n- `app/src/phone/PhoneApp.tsx` — added \"Motion sensor\" section with iOS requestPermission gate; wires the motion runner to `devicemotion` events and the DataChannel\n- `app/src/ui/PairScreen.tsx` — loads active profile, sends initial `config: armed` after peer connects, mounts CalibrateScreen\n\nTests: 147 → 173 (+26). Lint + typecheck + vite build all clean. Bundle: main bundle unchanged at 344.22 KB / 106.44 KB gzip (Phase 2b.2 baseline); PairScreen lazy chunk +3 KB raw / +0.85 KB gzip (CalibrateScreen); PhoneApp lazy chunk +3 KB raw / +0.99 KB gzip (motion+runner).\n\nVerify run (with `npm run dev` up): C1 pass (motion section renders, status=\"off\", buttons present); C2 pass (Enable motion → status=\"on\", zero console errors); C3 fail under best-effort (peer didn't reach `connected` in headless chromium without UDP — same documented §B2 path Phase 2b.2 took).\n\n**What I want you to evaluate:**\n\n1. **Correctness**: especially the motion math (`magnitudeG`, `createImpulseDetector` debounce semantics, `computeCalibrationThreshold` p10 + epsilon + floor) and the wire-format extension. Are there off-by-one or floating-point gotchas around the 300ms debounce or the threshold clamp?\n2. **CalibrateScreen state machine** (`idle → collecting → done` with `aborted` on failure): does it correctly handle peer.send throwing, partial-save scenarios, and rapid re-runs?\n3. **PhoneApp motion lifecycle**: are listeners cleaned up correctly? Is the iOS `requestPermission` path correctly gated only when the API exists? Does the `attachMotionListener` / `detachMotionListener` pair leak across StrictMode double-mounts?\n4. **Wire format**: is the type-narrowing for the union safe? Could a hostile config message smuggle bad values past `isPhoneMessage`?\n5. **DRY**: am I duplicating constants/logic that should be shared (e.g. `DEFAULT_THRESHOLD_G` lookup pattern in PairScreen vs PhoneApp)?\n6. **Security**: this is a LAN-trust model per §B1 — no auth on the DataChannel — but flag anything that goes beyond that risk envelope.\n\nReturn findings as a numbered list with severity (block/should-fix/nit) and a one-line rationale. Skip stylistic nits unless they affect correctness. Suggest the smallest correctness fixes inline."}
