[tool_result] toolu_01RMT7xjtomsx7Joh4ypQRoZ 1	# Setting up the phone companion on iOS
2	
3	PointFight Reactor's phone-as-sensor mode (`/phone`) works without any
4	configuration on **Android Chrome over plain HTTP** on your LAN. iOS
5	Safari is stricter: it refuses to grant `DeviceMotionEvent` permission
6	to any origin that is not served over HTTPS with a certificate the device
7	already trusts.
8	
9	This page is the one-time setup an iOS user runs on their laptop so that
10	`https://<laptop-lan-ip>:5173/phone` works on their iPhone.
11	
12	If you are on Android, **stop reading — you do not need any of this**.
13	
14	## Why
15	
16	iOS Safari (verified iOS 15–17) treats `DeviceMotionEvent.requestPermission`
17	as a privileged API and silently returns `denied` on insecure origins.
18	A self-signed certificate is not enough: iOS only honours the permission
19	prompt when the certificate's root is in the device's trust store. The
20	cheapest way to get a trusted LAN certificate is
21	[mkcert](https://github.com/FiloSottile/mkcert).
